---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **사라졌다 돌아오는 작품.** (`/returns`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **첫 눈에 보인 것이 함정이었다는 것을 화면에 적는다.** 제일 오래 비었다 돌아온 것이
 *    시즌 2 였다. 시즌 칸으로 안 가르면 「새 시즌」을 「복귀」로 파는 셈이다.
 * ⛔ **죽은 가설을 같이 싣는다.** 「새 시즌이 옛 시즌을 끌어올린다」는 5.2% 뿐이었다.
 * ⛔ **문턱이 우리 것임을 밝힌다.** 4주는 우리가 골랐다. 다른 문턱 표를 옆에 둔다.
 * ⛔ **못 가린 것을 「복귀 아님」으로 안 민다.** 시즌 칸이 21% 비어 있다.
 * ⛔ 왜 돌아왔는지는 이 자료에 **없다.**
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-returns.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-returns.json';

const TITLE = 'When a Korean title comes back to a chart it had left';
const DESC =
  `Across ${data.cellsMeasured.toLocaleString('en-US')} title-and-market cells, ${data.gaps} gaps of `
  + `${data.thresholdWeeks} weeks or more end with the title returning. ${data.seasonChangedPc}% of those `
  + `are a different season, not a return. Of the ${data.sameSeason} genuine returns, ${data.alonePc}% `
  + `happen with no other season of the same title on the chart.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/returns">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every other page here counts a title's chart life as one run — so many weeks, so many countries.
    It is not always one run. A title leaves a country's top 10 and, sometimes, comes back. This page
    is about how often that is real.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Most of what looks like a return is a new season</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <p class="warn">
      The first thing we saw was a title returning after <b>{Math.max(...data.longestReturns.map((x) => x.gapWeeks))}
      weeks</b> away, and the second thing we saw was that the obvious cases were second seasons.
      Netflix labels each chart row with a season, so we split the gaps on that label rather than
      guessing. It changes the answer.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Gaps of {data.thresholdWeeks} weeks or more</th><th>Cells</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">The season label changed — a new season, not a return</td>
            <td>{data.seasonChanged}</td><td class="tot">{data.seasonChangedPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Same season — the title genuinely came back</td>
            <td>{data.sameSeason}</td><td class="tot">{data.sameSeasonPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">No season label on one side — we cannot tell</td>
            <td>{data.unknownSeason}</td><td class="tot">{data.unknownSeasonPc}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>⚠ The third row is not a small print.</b> Netflix carries a season label on
      {' '}{data.seasonLabelledRows.toLocaleString('en-US')} of the
      {' '}{data.koreanRows.toLocaleString('en-US')} Korean chart rows between {data.weekFrom} and
      {' '}{data.weekTo} — {data.seasonLabelPc}% — so {data.unknownSeasonPc}% of gaps cannot be
      classified at all. We leave them in their own row rather than folding them into either answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The hypothesis we had, and had to drop</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      The obvious explanation for an old season charting again is that a new one arrived and pulled it
      back. We checked: for each genuine return, was another season of the same title on that chart
      within {data.nearbyWeeks} weeks either side? It was in <b>{data.pulledByOtherSeason} of
      {' '}{data.sameSeason} cases — {data.pulledByOtherSeasonPc}%</b>.
      {' '}<b>{data.alonePc}% of returns happen alone.</b>
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      That is the finding, and it is not the one we expected. Old Korean seasons re-enter charts
      without a new season beside them. What brings them back is not in this data — see the last
      section — but it is not, in the main, a sequel.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Four weeks is our threshold, so here are the others</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">{data.thresholdNote}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Gap of at least</th><th>Gaps</th><th>Season changed</th><th>Same season</th><th>Cannot tell</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.thresholds.map((t) => (
            <tr class={t.weeks === data.thresholdWeeks ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{t.weeks} weeks</td>
              <td>{t.gaps}</td>
              <td>{t.seasonChanged}</td>
              <td class="tot">{t.sameSeason}</td>
              <td>{t.unknown}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Read the third column down: it barely moves — {data.thresholds[0].seasonChanged} at two weeks,
      {' '}{data.thresholds[data.thresholds.length - 1].seasonChanged} at twelve. A gap between seasons
      is long by construction, so raising the threshold does not remove those cases. The fourth column
      falls by more than half over the same range. <b>Whatever threshold you pick, the long gaps are
      mostly seasons.</b>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How common is any of this</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Title-and-market cells measured</td><td class="tot">{data.cellsMeasured.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Cells with no gap at all — one unbroken run</td><td class="tot">{data.contiguousCells.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Distinct titles that returned somewhere</td><td class="tot">{data.returnTitles.length}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Markets in the data</td><td class="tot">{data.marketCount}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Returning is the exception. Most title-and-market cells are a single unbroken run, and the
      {' '}{data.returnTitles.length} titles that came back somewhere are a small part of the
      catalogue — which is why we are reporting a mechanism rather than a trend.
    </p>
  </section>

  {data.byMarketBand && (
    <section>
      <h2>Where returns happen — and the gradient that dissolved</h2>
      <p class="note lead-in">
        The obvious follow-up is whether comebacks cluster in the markets where Korean titles are
        already common. Counted per cell, they appear to: the rate roughly triples from the smallest
        Korean charts to the largest. <b>That reading does not survive one control.</b>
      </p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Korean share of the market</th><th>Markets</th><th>Cells</th>
              <th>Returns per cell</th><th>Returns per opportunity</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {data.byMarketBand.map((b) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
                <td>{b.markets}</td>
                <td>{b.cells.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
                <td>{b.perCellPc.toFixed(1)}%</td>
                <td class="tot">{b.perChancePc.toFixed(2)}%</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="warn">
        A market where Korean titles are common holds them on its chart for longer, and a longer run
        has more weeks in it — more <b>opportunities</b> for a gap to open. Divide by the number of
        adjacent week pairs rather than by the number of cells and the fourth column, which ranged
        from {Math.min(...data.byMarketBand.map((b) => b.perCellPc)).toFixed(1)}% to
        {' '}{Math.max(...data.byMarketBand.map((b) => b.perCellPc)).toFixed(1)}%, flattens to between
        {' '}{Math.min(...data.byMarketBand.map((b) => b.perChancePc)).toFixed(2)}% and
        {' '}{Math.max(...data.byMarketBand.map((b) => b.perChancePc)).toFixed(2)}%.
      </p>
      <p class="note fine">
        The last column is printed to two decimal places on purpose. Rounded to one, the first two
        bands both read <b>1.0%</b> and the flatness would look like a rounding accident rather than
        a measurement — the same collapse we are warning about, one digit further along.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        <b>Returning is about as likely per opportunity everywhere.</b> Had we published the per-cell
        column alone we would have sold a story about big markets reviving catalogues, and it would
        have been an artefact of run length. The collector now refuses to write if the per-opportunity
        figures spread by more than 1.5 points, so if that ever stops being true this page will stop
        building rather than quietly change its mind.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section>
    <h2>The longest gaps that were genuine returns</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th>Market</th><th>Weeks away</th><th>Season on both sides</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.longestReturns.map((x) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{x.title}</td><td>{x.market}</td>
              <td class="tot">{x.gapWeeks}</td><td class="fine">{x.season}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Alphabetical, not ranked.</b> These are the ten longest gaps that survived the season test,
      shown in name order because this is not a league of comebacks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/returns" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix and Wikidata</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>Why a title returned is not here.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>The season label is Netflix's, not ours.</b> Where it is missing we say we cannot tell
        rather than assume; where it is present we take it at face value.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Russia is excluded</b> throughout this site, because Netflix withdrew from it and the
        remaining weeks are not comparable with the others.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/titles">Every Korean title that has charted</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/staying-power">How long titles stay</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
